850a-add7-5189-ad79-a9f8fe7e7fce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535923 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, a vertex that lies at the origin. It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/c2/c2aa0f0b3bd88556e29ae859a9feae21332b58bb06dbe9e27ca7c1d639feb9e0.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535956 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535956 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535956 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535956 #edb/ref 1071 "How is the angle $-\\frac{13\\pi }{18}$ normally represented in the coordinate plane?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535956 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "69f2d31b-6c6d-5d78-bb33-d5bfc1d325a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535956 #edb/ref 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-(\\frac{13\\pi }{18})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=-{130}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle $-\\frac{13\\pi }{18}$ is equivalent to $-{130}^{∘}.$ \n\nNow, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and a vertex that lies at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/42/42c03656382d8caa36fc7937d47d31d2932e139a5282afb5e0787ea518130691.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535961 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535902 #edb/ref 1759218604541